5
00:00:42.955 --> 00:00:44.923
♪ ♪

6
00:00:58.971 --> 00:01:00.939
♪ ♪

7
00:01:05.978 --> 00:01:07.946
(birds chirping)

8
00:01:24.897 --> 00:01:26.832
(footsteps approaching)

9
00:01:29.668 --> 00:01:30.969
SANDERS:
Blake.

10
00:01:32.037 --> 00:01:34.137
Blake.

11
00:01:34.139 --> 00:01:35.972
Sorry. Sarge.

12
00:01:35.974 --> 00:01:38.008
Pick a man.

13
00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:39.810
Bring your kit.

14
00:01:39.812 --> 00:01:41.745
Yes. Sarge.

15
00:01:41.747 --> 00:01:42.981
(sighs)

16
00:01:49.154 --> 00:01:50.722
(sighs)

17
00:01:58.030 --> 00:01:59.598
(grunts softly)

18
00:02:03.102 --> 00:02:04.734
SANDERS:
Don't dawdle.

19
00:02:04.736 --> 00:02:06.071
No. Sarge.

20
00:02:15.047 --> 00:02:17.047
Did they feed us?

21
00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:20.717
No. Just mail.

22
00:02:20.719 --> 00:02:21.985
(bell clangs)

23
00:02:21.987 --> 00:02:23.687
MAN (in distance):
Time for some tea!

24
00:02:23.689 --> 00:02:25.157
- (bell clangs)
- Tea's up!

25
00:02:37.236 --> 00:02:40.103
(chuckles softly)

26
00:02:40.105 --> 00:02:42.040
Myrtle's having puppies.

27
00:02:44.743 --> 00:02:46.176
You get anything?

28
00:02:46.178 --> 00:02:49.012
No.

29
00:02:49.014 --> 00:02:52.082
Oh. I'm bloody starving.
Aren't you?

30
00:02:52.084 --> 00:02:55.085
Thought we might get
some decent grub out here.

31
00:02:55.087 --> 00:02:58.056
It was the only reason I
decided against the priesthood.

32
00:03:03.795 --> 00:03:05.095
What you got there?

33
00:03:05.097 --> 00:03:06.930
Ham and bread.

34
00:03:06.932 --> 00:03:09.065
Where did you find that?

35
00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:11.101
I have my uses.

36
00:03:11.103 --> 00:03:13.136
- (bell clangs)
- Here.

37
00:03:13.138 --> 00:03:14.840
(bell clangs)

38
00:03:17.776 --> 00:03:19.109
Ugh.

39
00:03:19.111 --> 00:03:21.077
Tastes like old shoe.

40
00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:22.513
Cheer up.

41
00:03:22.515 --> 00:03:25.782
This time next week.
it'll be chicken dinner.

42
00:03:25.784 --> 00:03:28.018
Not me.

43
00:03:28.020 --> 00:03:30.086
My leave got canceled.

44
00:03:30.088 --> 00:03:32.222
They say why?

45
00:03:32.224 --> 00:03:35.091
No idea.

46
00:03:35.093 --> 00:03:38.128
It's easier
not to go back at all.

47
00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.230
(men shouting orders
in distance)

48
00:03:40.232 --> 00:03:41.967
MAN:
Coming through.

49
00:03:45.037 --> 00:03:48.138
(men continue shouting orders
in distance)

50
00:03:48.140 --> 00:03:49.839
Something's up.

51
00:03:49.841 --> 00:03:53.243
- Did you hear anything?
- No.

52
00:03:53.245 --> 00:03:56.813
Has to be the push. right?

53
00:03:56.815 --> 00:03:59.216
Ten bob says we're going up.

54
00:03:59.218 --> 00:04:01.351
I'm not taking that bet.

55
00:04:01.353 --> 00:04:03.253
Why? 'Cause you know I'm right?

56
00:04:03.255 --> 00:04:05.389
No. Because you haven't
got ten bob.

57
00:04:05.391 --> 00:04:07.092
(chuckles)

58
00:04:10.162 --> 00:04:12.796
In your own time. gentlemen.

59
00:04:12.798 --> 00:04:14.130
BLAKE:
Is there any news. Sarge?

60
00:04:14.132 --> 00:04:16.233
- SANDERS: News of what?
- BLAKE: The big push.

61
00:04:16.235 --> 00:04:17.867
It was supposed to happen
weeks ago.

62
00:04:17.869 --> 00:04:19.570
They told us we'd be home
by Christmas.

63
00:04:19.572 --> 00:04:21.338
SANDERS: Yes. well. sorry to
disrupt your crowded schedule.

64
00:04:21.340 --> 00:04:23.873
Blake. but the brass hats
didn't fancy it in the snow.

65
00:04:23.875 --> 00:04:25.041
BLAKE:
More's the pity. Sarge.

66
00:04:25.043 --> 00:04:26.209
I could've done with
some turkey.

67
00:04:26.211 --> 00:04:27.811
SANDERS:
Well. I'll make sure

68
00:04:27.813 --> 00:04:29.946
to relay your displeasure
to command.

69
00:04:29.948 --> 00:04:31.582
SCHOFIELD: So what's on
the cards. then. Sergeant?

70
00:04:31.584 --> 00:04:33.149
SANDERS:
The Hun are up to something.

71
00:04:33.151 --> 00:04:34.884
SCHOFIELD:
Any idea what?

72
00:04:34.886 --> 00:04:37.823
SANDERS: No. But it's
about to ruin our weekend.

73
00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:46.263
Now. listen.

74
00:04:46.265 --> 00:04:50.066
Erinmore is inside.
so tidy yourselves up.

75
00:04:50.068 --> 00:04:51.368
Never know.

76
00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.971
Might be mentions in dispatches
for this one.

77
00:04:54.973 --> 00:04:56.908
If you don't bugger it up.

78
00:04:59.978 --> 00:05:02.180
Must be something big
if the general's here.

79
00:05:12.291 --> 00:05:14.993
Lance Corporals
Blake and Schofield. sir.

80
00:05:17.095 --> 00:05:19.062
Which one of you is Blake?

81
00:05:19.064 --> 00:05:20.897
BLAKE:
Sir.

82
00:05:20.899 --> 00:05:24.034
You have a brother.
a lieutenant in the 2nd Devons?

83
00:05:24.036 --> 00:05:25.101
Yes. sir.

84
00:05:25.103 --> 00:05:27.170
- Joseph Blake. Is he...
- Alive.

85
00:05:27.172 --> 00:05:29.205
As far as I know.

86
00:05:29.207 --> 00:05:32.175
And with your help.
I'd like to keep it that way.

87
00:05:32.177 --> 00:05:34.077
Sanders tells me
you're good with maps.

88
00:05:34.079 --> 00:05:35.479
That true?

89
00:05:35.481 --> 00:05:38.048
Good enough. sir.

90
00:05:38.050 --> 00:05:41.051
So...

91
00:05:41.053 --> 00:05:43.987
we are here.

92
00:05:43.989 --> 00:05:47.290
The 2nd Devons
are advancing here.

93
00:05:47.292 --> 00:05:49.393
How long will it take you
to get there?

94
00:05:49.395 --> 00:05:50.894
♪ ♪

95
00:05:50.896 --> 00:05:52.295
I don't understand. sir.

96
00:05:52.297 --> 00:05:54.230
SCHOFIELD: Sir. that land
is held by the Germans.

97
00:05:54.232 --> 00:05:56.366
Germans have gone.

98
00:05:56.368 --> 00:05:58.301
Don't get your hopes up.

99
00:05:58.303 --> 00:06:01.971
It appears to be
a strategic withdrawal.

100
00:06:01.973 --> 00:06:03.507
They seem to have
created a new line.

101
00:06:03.509 --> 00:06:06.309
nine miles back here
by the looks of it.

102
00:06:06.311 --> 00:06:09.913
Colonel Mackenzie
is in command of the 2nd.

103
00:06:09.915 --> 00:06:11.247
He sent word yesterday morning

104
00:06:11.249 --> 00:06:14.284
he was going after
the retreating Germans.

105
00:06:14.286 --> 00:06:16.487
He is convinced
he has them on the run.

106
00:06:16.489 --> 00:06:18.355
That if he can
break their lines now.

107
00:06:18.357 --> 00:06:21.257
he will turn the tide.

108
00:06:21.259 --> 00:06:23.026
He is wrong.

109
00:06:23.028 --> 00:06:26.029
Colonel Mackenzie
has not seen these aerials

110
00:06:26.031 --> 00:06:27.398
of the enemy's new line.

111
00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:29.267
Come around here. gentlemen.

112
00:06:31.303 --> 00:06:34.337
Three miles deep.
field fortifications. defenses.

113
00:06:34.339 --> 00:06:38.141
artillery the like of which
we've never seen before.

114
00:06:38.143 --> 00:06:40.043
The 2nd are due
to attack the line

115
00:06:40.045 --> 00:06:42.245
shortly after dawn tomorrow.

116
00:06:42.247 --> 00:06:44.415
They have no idea
what they're in for.

117
00:06:44.417 --> 00:06:46.249
And we can't warn them.

118
00:06:46.251 --> 00:06:50.286
As a parting gift. the enemy
cut all our telephone lines.

119
00:06:50.288 --> 00:06:53.356
Your orders are to get
to the 2nd at Croisilles Wood.

120
00:06:53.358 --> 00:06:57.026
one mile southeast
of the town of Écoust.

121
00:06:57.028 --> 00:06:59.028
Deliver this
to Colonel Mackenzie.

122
00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.433
It is a direct order to call
off tomorrow morning's attack.

123
00:07:02.435 --> 00:07:05.335
If you don't.
it will be a massacre.

124
00:07:05.337 --> 00:07:07.270
We will lose two battalions...

125
00:07:07.272 --> 00:07:10.574
1.600 men.
your brother among them.

126
00:07:10.576 --> 00:07:13.042
You think you can
get there in time?

127
00:07:13.044 --> 00:07:14.077
Yes. sir.

128
00:07:14.079 --> 00:07:15.345
Any questions?

129
00:07:15.347 --> 00:07:17.213
- No. sir.
- Good.

130
00:07:17.215 --> 00:07:19.050
Over to you. Lieutenant.

131
00:07:21.454 --> 00:07:24.254
GORDON:
Supplies. gentlemen.

132
00:07:24.256 --> 00:07:29.192
Map. torches. grenades. and.
uh. a couple of little treats.

133
00:07:29.194 --> 00:07:30.561
Leave immediately.

134
00:07:30.563 --> 00:07:33.029
Take this trench west
up on Sauchiehall Street.

135
00:07:33.031 --> 00:07:35.198
then northwest on
Paradise Alley at the front.

136
00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:38.168
Continue along the front line
until you find the Yorks.

137
00:07:38.170 --> 00:07:40.003
Give this note
to Major Stevenson.

138
00:07:40.005 --> 00:07:42.338
He's holding the line at the
shortest span of no-man's-land.

139
00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:44.340
You'll cross there.

140
00:07:44.342 --> 00:07:46.042
It'll be daylight. sir.
They'll see us.

141
00:07:46.044 --> 00:07:47.378
ERINMORE: There's no need
to be concerned.

142
00:07:47.380 --> 00:07:49.347
You should meet no resistance.

143
00:07:56.054 --> 00:07:59.355
Sir. is... is it just us?

144
00:07:59.357 --> 00:08:01.559
"Down to Gehenna
or up to the Throne.

145
00:08:01.561 --> 00:08:04.795
He travels the fastest
who travels alone."

146
00:08:04.797 --> 00:08:06.229
Wouldn't you say. Lieutenant?

147
00:08:06.231 --> 00:08:07.966
GORDON:
Yes. sir. I would.

148
00:08:10.035 --> 00:08:11.369
Good luck.

149
00:08:21.246 --> 00:08:23.447
Blake. let's talk about this
for a minute.

150
00:08:23.449 --> 00:08:25.416
Why?

151
00:08:25.418 --> 00:08:27.050
(sighs)

152
00:08:27.052 --> 00:08:29.587
♪ ♪

153
00:08:29.589 --> 00:08:32.288
(men chattering quietly)

154
00:08:32.290 --> 00:08:33.992
Blake.

155
00:08:40.332 --> 00:08:42.131
We just need to think about it.

156
00:08:42.133 --> 00:08:43.299
BLAKE:
There's nothing to think about.

157
00:08:43.301 --> 00:08:44.435
It's my big brother.

158
00:08:44.437 --> 00:08:46.269
SCHOFIELD: We should at least
wait until it's dark.

159
00:08:46.271 --> 00:08:47.671
BLAKE: Erinmore said
to leave immediately.

160
00:08:47.673 --> 00:08:50.139
SCHOFIELD: Erinmore's
never seen no-man's-land.

161
00:08:50.141 --> 00:08:51.842
We won't make it ten yards.

162
00:08:51.844 --> 00:08:53.844
- If we just wait...
- BLAKE: You heard him.

163
00:08:53.846 --> 00:08:55.846
He said the Boche have gone.

164
00:08:55.848 --> 00:08:58.314
SCHOFIELD: Is that why
he gave us grenades?

165
00:08:58.316 --> 00:09:00.285
(busy chatter)

166
00:09:04.289 --> 00:09:06.024
- Oy.
- (grunts. exhales sharply)

167
00:09:08.360 --> 00:09:10.226
- (grunts)
- MAN: Watch where you're going.

168
00:09:10.228 --> 00:09:11.495
SCHOFIELD:
Sorry.

169
00:09:11.497 --> 00:09:13.298
(panting)

170
00:09:15.367 --> 00:09:16.569
(exhales sharply)

171
00:09:20.305 --> 00:09:22.606
All I'm saying is that we wait.

172
00:09:22.608 --> 00:09:26.177
Yes. you would say that. 'cause
it's not your brother. is it?

173
00:09:28.447 --> 00:09:30.681
Look. the last time I was told
the Germans were gone.

174
00:09:30.683 --> 00:09:33.316
it didn't end well.

175
00:09:33.318 --> 00:09:35.521
You don't know. Blake.
You weren't there.

176
00:09:38.156 --> 00:09:40.491
- Excuse me. Excuse me.
- (busy chatter)

177
00:09:40.493 --> 00:09:42.060
Excuse me.

178
00:09:46.399 --> 00:09:48.399
You're going up the
down trench. you bloody idiot.

179
00:09:48.401 --> 00:09:50.335
Orders of the general. sir.

180
00:09:51.437 --> 00:09:52.638
(grunts)

181
00:10:02.615 --> 00:10:05.482
All right.
Say the Boche have gone.

182
00:10:05.484 --> 00:10:07.484
Nine miles will take us.
what. six hours?

183
00:10:07.486 --> 00:10:09.218
Eight at the very most.

184
00:10:09.220 --> 00:10:11.220
So we've got time to wait
until the sun sets.

185
00:10:11.222 --> 00:10:12.656
- It's enemy territory.
- Otherwise. we'll be wide open.

186
00:10:12.658 --> 00:10:14.525
We've got no idea
what we're walking into.

187
00:10:14.527 --> 00:10:17.226
Blake. if we're not
clever about this.

188
00:10:17.228 --> 00:10:19.597
no one will
get to your brother.

189
00:10:19.599 --> 00:10:21.366
I will.

190
00:10:23.402 --> 00:10:25.504
We're here.
This is the front line.

191
00:10:28.173 --> 00:10:30.173
Now we need to find the Yorks.

192
00:10:30.175 --> 00:10:32.110
♪ ♪

193
00:10:49.327 --> 00:10:51.327
(men chattering quietly)

194
00:10:51.329 --> 00:10:53.264
♪ ♪

195
00:11:14.754 --> 00:11:16.520
- BLAKE: Sorry. mate.
- MAN: Jesus!

196
00:11:16.522 --> 00:11:17.521
STOKES:
Watch who you're shoving.

197
00:11:17.523 --> 00:11:19.288
BLAKE: All right.
get out of the way. then.

198
00:11:19.290 --> 00:11:20.557
STOKES: Hey. who the fuck
do you think you are.

199
00:11:20.559 --> 00:11:21.558
pushing wounded soldiers
around?!

200
00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:22.826
BLAKE:
Let go! Let go of me!

201
00:11:22.828 --> 00:11:24.428
Arsehole knocked down
our sergeant!

202
00:11:24.430 --> 00:11:25.429
The man's fucking wounded!

203
00:11:25.431 --> 00:11:27.431
All right. I'm sorry.
All right? I'm sorry!

204
00:11:27.433 --> 00:11:28.632
- We're on commission.
- Just let me through.

205
00:11:28.634 --> 00:11:32.301
- Orders from the general.
- Just let me through. (panting)

206
00:11:32.303 --> 00:11:35.238
Get out of the way.

207
00:11:35.240 --> 00:11:36.507
Fine.

208
00:11:36.509 --> 00:11:38.307
Just watch where you're going.

209
00:11:38.309 --> 00:11:40.245
(panting)

210
00:11:55.494 --> 00:11:56.729
(sighs):
Yorks.

211
00:12:02.635 --> 00:12:04.202
(dog barks in distance)

212
00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:09.640
It's bloody quiet.

213
00:12:09.642 --> 00:12:12.376
Was it like this
before Thiepval?

214
00:12:12.378 --> 00:12:14.343
Don't remember.

215
00:12:14.345 --> 00:12:16.312
You don't remember the Somme?

216
00:12:16.314 --> 00:12:17.648
- Not really.
- MAN: Hey. mind us.

217
00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:19.350
Come on.

218
00:12:20.753 --> 00:12:23.554
Well. you did all right
out of it.

219
00:12:23.556 --> 00:12:25.889
At least wear your ribbon.

220
00:12:25.891 --> 00:12:28.492
Don't have it anymore.

221
00:12:28.494 --> 00:12:30.861
What? You lost your medal?

222
00:12:30.863 --> 00:12:33.564
MAN:
Heads down. boys.

223
00:12:33.566 --> 00:12:36.367
MAN:
Come on. lads. Keep digging.

224
00:12:39.772 --> 00:12:41.474
Stay low.

225
00:12:48.313 --> 00:12:49.680
For God's sake. Careful there.

226
00:12:49.682 --> 00:12:51.515
You're stepping on the dead.

227
00:12:51.517 --> 00:12:53.383
That's our sergeant.

228
00:12:53.385 --> 00:12:55.686
Be better washing them out of
this dugout with a bloody hose.

229
00:12:55.688 --> 00:12:57.488
Do you know
where the Yorks are?

230
00:12:57.490 --> 00:12:58.422
The next bend.

231
00:12:58.424 --> 00:13:00.323
you'll be standing
on top of half of them.

232
00:13:00.325 --> 00:13:01.859
Shot to hell two nights ago.

233
00:13:01.861 --> 00:13:03.796
(panting)

234
00:13:10.936 --> 00:13:13.670
- SCHOFIELD: Yorks?
- Yes. Corp.

235
00:13:13.672 --> 00:13:15.506
BLAKE:
Where's Major Stevenson?

236
00:13:15.508 --> 00:13:18.542
He was killed.
couple of nights ago. Corporal.

237
00:13:18.544 --> 00:13:20.544
Lieutenant Leslie has command.

238
00:13:20.546 --> 00:13:22.446
Where can we find him?

239
00:13:22.448 --> 00:13:24.448
Next dugout.

240
00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:26.418
(crows cawing)

241
00:13:31.457 --> 00:13:32.691
Here.

242
00:13:37.797 --> 00:13:39.530
BLAKE:
Sir?

243
00:13:39.532 --> 00:13:41.732
- Lieutenant Leslie. sir?
- What is it?

244
00:13:41.734 --> 00:13:43.634
We have a message
from General Erinmore.

245
00:13:43.636 --> 00:13:45.502
- Are you our relief?
- No. sir.

246
00:13:45.504 --> 00:13:47.538
Well. when the fucking hell
are they due?

247
00:13:47.540 --> 00:13:49.940
We don't know. sir. but we've
got orders to cross here.

248
00:13:49.942 --> 00:13:51.742
That is the German front line.

249
00:13:51.744 --> 00:13:53.443
We know. sir.

250
00:13:53.445 --> 00:13:55.414
If you'll just take the letter.

251
00:13:57.449 --> 00:13:59.883
Settle a bet.
What day is it?

252
00:13:59.885 --> 00:14:01.785
- Friday.
- Friday. Well. well. well.

253
00:14:01.787 --> 00:14:02.953
None of us was right.

254
00:14:02.955 --> 00:14:04.755
This idiot thought
it was Tuesday.

255
00:14:04.757 --> 00:14:07.524
- MAN: Sorry. sir.
- (man coughs)

256
00:14:07.526 --> 00:14:09.726
Are they out of
their fucking minds?

257
00:14:09.728 --> 00:14:12.796
One slow night. the brass think
the Hun have just gone home.

258
00:14:12.798 --> 00:14:15.732
SCHOFIELD: Do you think
they're wrong. sir?

259
00:14:15.734 --> 00:14:18.001
We lost an officer
and three men two nights ago.

260
00:14:18.003 --> 00:14:19.870
They were shot to bits
patching up wire.

261
00:14:19.872 --> 00:14:22.773
We dragged two of them
back here. Needn't bothered.

262
00:14:22.775 --> 00:14:25.943
Sir. the general is sure
the enemy have withdrawn.

263
00:14:25.945 --> 00:14:27.744
There are aerials
of the new line that...

264
00:14:27.746 --> 00:14:28.912
Shut up.
We fought and died

265
00:14:28.914 --> 00:14:30.747
over every inch
of this fucking place.

266
00:14:30.749 --> 00:14:32.416
Now they suddenly
give us miles?

267
00:14:32.418 --> 00:14:34.352
It's a trap.

268
00:14:36.922 --> 00:14:39.189
But chin up. There's a medal
in it. for sure.

269
00:14:39.191 --> 00:14:42.360
Nothing like a scrap of ribbon
to cheer up a widow.

270
00:14:44.797 --> 00:14:46.496
All right.
(clears throat)

271
00:14:46.498 --> 00:14:47.633
(man coughing)

272
00:14:48.901 --> 00:14:50.701
Where's the nearest
way through. sir?

273
00:14:50.703 --> 00:14:53.737
Our wire's a mess. but there is
a path through of sorts.

274
00:14:53.739 --> 00:14:55.873
Rushworth. let him look.

275
00:14:55.875 --> 00:14:57.810
(crows cawing)

276
00:15:00.813 --> 00:15:02.913
Straight ahead to the left.
past the dead horses.

277
00:15:02.915 --> 00:15:05.782
There's a gap
directly behind them.

278
00:15:05.784 --> 00:15:08.752
Useful. because if it's dark.
you follow the stench.

279
00:15:08.754 --> 00:15:10.654
When you get
to the second wire.

280
00:15:10.656 --> 00:15:12.656
look out for the bowing chap.

281
00:15:12.658 --> 00:15:14.691
There's a small break
just beside him.

282
00:15:14.693 --> 00:15:17.995
The German line is
150-odd yards after that.

283
00:15:17.997 --> 00:15:20.831
Watch out for the craters;
they're deeper than they look.

284
00:15:20.833 --> 00:15:22.933
You fall in.
there's no getting out.

285
00:15:22.935 --> 00:15:24.003
This way.

286
00:15:26.071 --> 00:15:27.804
Wake up. Kilgour.

287
00:15:27.806 --> 00:15:29.072
Bloody waste of space.

288
00:15:29.074 --> 00:15:31.475
Any cover. sir?
Anywhere to jump off from?

289
00:15:31.477 --> 00:15:33.644
No. the sap trench
was blown to hell weeks ago.

290
00:15:33.646 --> 00:15:34.912
It was full of bodies anyway.

291
00:15:34.914 --> 00:15:37.616
Your best bet
is to pop over here.

292
00:15:40.653 --> 00:15:43.620
If you do get shot. try to
make it back to the wire.

293
00:15:43.622 --> 00:15:45.555
We won't come after you.
not till it's dark.

294
00:15:45.557 --> 00:15:48.592
And if by some fucking miracle
you do make it.

295
00:15:48.594 --> 00:15:49.726
send up a flare.

296
00:15:49.728 --> 00:15:51.762
Don't have any. sir.

297
00:15:51.764 --> 00:15:53.897
Well. get him one. Kilgour.
Make yourself useful.

298
00:15:53.899 --> 00:15:55.499
Yes. sir.

299
00:15:55.501 --> 00:15:56.502
(Blake grunts quietly)

300
00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:00.070
Through this holy unction.

301
00:16:00.072 --> 00:16:01.772
may the Lord
pardon you your faults

302
00:16:01.774 --> 00:16:03.842
and whatever sins
thou has committed.

303
00:16:06.845 --> 00:16:08.946
I do hate losing these
to the Hun.

304
00:16:08.948 --> 00:16:11.548
so when they start
shooting at you.

305
00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:13.617
could you be so kind
as to throw it back?

306
00:16:13.619 --> 00:16:14.987
There's a good chap.

307
00:16:17.957 --> 00:16:19.957
Cheerio.

308
00:16:19.959 --> 00:16:21.894
(Blake grunting quietly)

309
00:16:26.865 --> 00:16:28.932
You sure?

310
00:16:28.934 --> 00:16:30.836
Yes.

311
00:16:36.809 --> 00:16:38.744
Age before beauty.

312
00:16:39.812 --> 00:16:41.747
♪ ♪

313
00:17:01.834 --> 00:17:03.769
♪ ♪

314
00:17:08.841 --> 00:17:10.776
(flies buzzing)

315
00:17:30.863 --> 00:17:32.798
(flies continue buzzing)

316
00:17:52.885 --> 00:17:54.820
♪ ♪

317
00:18:14.039 --> 00:18:15.974
(metal creaking)

318
00:18:19.044 --> 00:18:20.612
(grunts)

319
00:18:21.814 --> 00:18:23.749
(panting)

320
00:18:25.751 --> 00:18:27.119
You all right?

321
00:18:29.721 --> 00:18:31.056
Look for cover.

322
00:18:45.771 --> 00:18:46.903
(grunts)

323
00:18:46.905 --> 00:18:48.874
(panting)

324
00:18:52.945 --> 00:18:54.646
Sap trench.

325
00:19:06.225 --> 00:19:08.160
(panting)

326
00:19:14.733 --> 00:19:15.934
(both grunt)

327
00:19:25.844 --> 00:19:27.779
(footsteps splashing)

328
00:19:39.725 --> 00:19:41.660
(footsteps splashing)

329
00:19:50.269 --> 00:19:52.204
(grunting)

330
00:20:12.291 --> 00:20:14.291
♪ ♪

331
00:20:14.293 --> 00:20:16.862
(planes approaching)

332
00:20:21.934 --> 00:20:23.800
Stay still.

333
00:20:23.802 --> 00:20:25.737
(plane engines roaring)

334
00:20:31.944 --> 00:20:34.878
They're ours.

335
00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:36.982
Keep going. We're halfway.

336
00:20:57.369 --> 00:20:59.304
♪ ♪

337
00:21:16.888 --> 00:21:18.824
(crows cawing)

338
00:21:35.374 --> 00:21:37.309
(crows cawing in distance)

339
00:21:41.381 --> 00:21:43.315
BLAKE:
There's a gap. in the wire.

340
00:21:46.885 --> 00:21:48.186
(grunts)

341
00:22:08.273 --> 00:22:10.208
♪ ♪

342
00:22:30.295 --> 00:22:32.230
♪ ♪

343
00:22:35.300 --> 00:22:36.299
(grunts)

344
00:22:36.301 --> 00:22:38.236
(panting)

345
00:22:41.441 --> 00:22:43.376
Sco.

346
00:22:45.077 --> 00:22:46.912
(grunts)

347
00:23:06.998 --> 00:23:09.132
There. That's the front line.

348
00:23:09.134 --> 00:23:11.069
♪ ♪

349
00:23:31.557 --> 00:23:34.425
(both panting)

350
00:23:34.427 --> 00:23:36.359
Fuck me.

351
00:23:36.361 --> 00:23:38.063
They really have gone.

352
00:23:59.184 --> 00:24:02.185
Your hand all right?

353
00:24:02.187 --> 00:24:05.456
Put it through
an effing German.

354
00:24:05.458 --> 00:24:07.223
Patch it up.

355
00:24:07.225 --> 00:24:10.428
You'll be wanking again
in no time.

356
00:24:10.430 --> 00:24:12.364
Wrong hand.

357
00:24:17.068 --> 00:24:19.037
(birds calling in distance)

358
00:24:38.558 --> 00:24:40.493
(sizzling)

359
00:24:42.294 --> 00:24:44.294
They're not long gone.

360
00:24:44.296 --> 00:24:46.231
♪ ♪

361
00:25:07.319 --> 00:25:09.020
SCHOFIELD:
No good.

362
00:25:31.109 --> 00:25:33.109
Blocked.

363
00:25:33.111 --> 00:25:35.046
This might be a way through.

364
00:25:44.557 --> 00:25:46.124
(switches click)

365
00:25:50.328 --> 00:25:52.030
Jesus.

366
00:26:00.171 --> 00:26:02.606
Look at this.

367
00:26:02.608 --> 00:26:04.309
It's massive.

368
00:26:07.279 --> 00:26:09.214
They built all this.

369
00:26:29.301 --> 00:26:31.236
♪ ♪

370
00:26:51.323 --> 00:26:53.258
♪ ♪

371
00:26:55.494 --> 00:26:57.360
Here's our way through.

372
00:26:57.362 --> 00:26:59.663
BLAKE:
Sco.

373
00:26:59.665 --> 00:27:01.297
How about this?

374
00:27:01.299 --> 00:27:03.368
- (springs creaking)
- (chuckles)

375
00:27:06.171 --> 00:27:07.506
Bloody hell.

376
00:27:10.443 --> 00:27:12.378
Even their rats
are bigger than ours.

377
00:27:19.452 --> 00:27:22.419
What do you think's
in the bags?

378
00:27:22.421 --> 00:27:24.390
SCHOFIELD:
You cannot be that hungry.

379
00:27:30.429 --> 00:27:34.364
BLAKE: Look at him.
cocky little bastard.

380
00:27:34.366 --> 00:27:36.265
You could eat this. though.

381
00:27:36.267 --> 00:27:38.504
- What is it?
- Boche dog meat.

382
00:27:40.539 --> 00:27:42.508
BLAKE:
What's in the other boxes?

383
00:27:44.543 --> 00:27:47.645
- What's wrong?
- Trip wire.

384
00:27:47.647 --> 00:27:49.813
Don't move.

385
00:27:49.815 --> 00:27:51.584
Where is it?

386
00:27:53.653 --> 00:27:55.588
Goes from here to the door.

387
00:27:58.391 --> 00:27:59.556
Oh. Jesus!

388
00:27:59.558 --> 00:28:01.159
BLAKE:
Oh. no.

389
00:28:04.997 --> 00:28:07.165
(coughing)

390
00:28:11.302 --> 00:28:12.503
Sco!

391
00:28:12.505 --> 00:28:14.505
(coughing)

392
00:28:14.507 --> 00:28:16.305
(Schofield groaning. muffled)

393
00:28:16.307 --> 00:28:18.575
- (low rumbling)
- (wood creaking)

394
00:28:18.577 --> 00:28:20.577
- Sco?
- (Schofield grunts. muffled)

395
00:28:20.579 --> 00:28:23.847
- (wood crackling)
- (low rumbling continues)

396
00:28:23.849 --> 00:28:25.716
Sco!

397
00:28:25.718 --> 00:28:28.018
(muffled grunting)

398
00:28:28.020 --> 00:28:30.353
Sco! Sco.

399
00:28:30.355 --> 00:28:32.323
- (grunting)
- (wood creaking)

400
00:28:36.796 --> 00:28:40.296
Sco! Sco.

401
00:28:40.298 --> 00:28:42.466
(Blake panting)

402
00:28:42.468 --> 00:28:43.701
(sobs):
Sco.

403
00:28:43.703 --> 00:28:46.503
Sco! Wake up!

404
00:28:46.505 --> 00:28:47.671
Wake up!

405
00:28:47.673 --> 00:28:50.040
(Blake grunting)

406
00:28:50.042 --> 00:28:51.575
Sco! Sco!

407
00:28:51.577 --> 00:28:53.744
(coughing)

408
00:28:53.746 --> 00:28:56.345
- (gasping)
- Stand up! Stand up!

409
00:28:56.347 --> 00:28:58.882
- (coughing. gasping)
- Come on. Stand up!

410
00:28:58.884 --> 00:29:02.453
- Up! Up! Up!
- (grunting)

411
00:29:02.455 --> 00:29:03.754
- Come on!
- (panting)

412
00:29:03.756 --> 00:29:06.423
- Stand up!
- (coughing)

413
00:29:06.425 --> 00:29:09.560
(rumbling grows louder)

414
00:29:09.562 --> 00:29:10.861
The whole thing's coming down!

415
00:29:10.863 --> 00:29:12.696
- (coughing)
- Come on.

416
00:29:12.698 --> 00:29:14.498
(panting)

417
00:29:14.500 --> 00:29:17.601
(grunting)

418
00:29:17.603 --> 00:29:19.703
You keep hold of me!

419
00:29:19.705 --> 00:29:20.771
(coughs)

420
00:29:20.773 --> 00:29:22.539
(grunting)

421
00:29:22.541 --> 00:29:24.510
(rumbling continues)

422
00:29:32.785 --> 00:29:34.685
We need to keep moving!

423
00:29:34.687 --> 00:29:36.086
Come on!

424
00:29:36.088 --> 00:29:39.355
I can't see. I can't see!

425
00:29:39.357 --> 00:29:41.391
- (metal object falling)
- Oh! Stop. stop. stop. stop!

426
00:29:41.393 --> 00:29:42.593
Stop!

427
00:29:42.595 --> 00:29:44.828
It's a mine shaft!

428
00:29:44.830 --> 00:29:46.897
Right. we'll have to jump.

429
00:29:46.899 --> 00:29:48.266
Come on!

430
00:29:50.102 --> 00:29:51.334
(grunts)

431
00:29:51.336 --> 00:29:52.603
You're going to have to jump.

432
00:29:52.605 --> 00:29:54.370
- Just jump!
- I can't.

433
00:29:54.372 --> 00:29:56.607
- I can't see!
- You need to trust me.

434
00:29:56.609 --> 00:29:59.510
- Jump!
- (grunts)

435
00:29:59.512 --> 00:30:01.512
(both grunting. panting)

436
00:30:01.514 --> 00:30:04.515
(rumbling continues)

437
00:30:04.517 --> 00:30:07.453
Don't let go of me!
Don't let go!

438
00:30:10.623 --> 00:30:12.890
Light. There's light.

439
00:30:12.892 --> 00:30:14.827
(rumbling continues)

440
00:30:18.864 --> 00:30:20.833
(grunting)

441
00:30:24.537 --> 00:30:27.504
SCHOFIELD:
Stop. Stop.

442
00:30:27.506 --> 00:30:28.772
Just let me stand.

443
00:30:28.774 --> 00:30:31.875
(coughing)

444
00:30:31.877 --> 00:30:33.846
- (groans)
- Dirty bastards!

445
00:30:38.651 --> 00:30:40.450
- (rumbling fades)
- (coughing)

446
00:30:40.452 --> 00:30:43.453
Careful. They may have
left other traps.

447
00:30:43.455 --> 00:30:45.455
(Schofield grunting)

448
00:30:45.457 --> 00:30:47.426
♪ ♪

449
00:30:50.930 --> 00:30:52.498
Jesus.

450
00:30:53.799 --> 00:30:55.734
(both panting)

451
00:31:01.574 --> 00:31:03.473
(canteen opens)

452
00:31:03.475 --> 00:31:04.741
Dust.

453
00:31:04.743 --> 00:31:06.810
So much dust in my eyes.

454
00:31:06.812 --> 00:31:07.746
(grunts)

455
00:31:09.582 --> 00:31:12.482
(panting sharply)

456
00:31:12.484 --> 00:31:14.017
(grunts)

457
00:31:14.019 --> 00:31:15.619
(sighs)

458
00:31:15.621 --> 00:31:17.187
Here.

459
00:31:17.189 --> 00:31:19.357
Have some of mine.

460
00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:27.831
I wish I'd shot that rat now.

461
00:31:27.833 --> 00:31:30.834
I wish you picked
some other bloody idiot.

462
00:31:30.836 --> 00:31:34.438
- What?
- Why in God's name did you have to choose me?

463
00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:36.573
Well. I didn't know
what I was picking you for.

464
00:31:36.575 --> 00:31:37.975
No. you didn't.

465
00:31:37.977 --> 00:31:40.679
You never know.
That's your problem.

466
00:31:43.949 --> 00:31:46.583
All right. then. go back.

467
00:31:46.585 --> 00:31:47.784
Nothing's stopping you.

468
00:31:47.786 --> 00:31:50.754
- You can go all the way bloody home if you want.
- Don't.

469
00:31:50.756 --> 00:31:53.724
Just don't.

470
00:31:53.726 --> 00:31:55.926
I-I didn't know
what I was picking you for.

471
00:31:55.928 --> 00:31:58.629
I thought they were going to
send us back up the line

472
00:31:58.631 --> 00:32:00.998
or for food or something.

473
00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.667
I thought it was going to be
something easy.

474
00:32:03.669 --> 00:32:06.236
All right?

475
00:32:06.238 --> 00:32:08.407
I never thought
it would be this.

476
00:32:14.847 --> 00:32:16.615
So do you want to go back?

477
00:32:20.686 --> 00:32:22.621
Just fire the fucking flare.

478
00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:38.971
Up yours. Lieutenant.

479
00:32:43.943 --> 00:32:45.878
(flare pops. sizzles)

480
00:32:51.550 --> 00:32:52.885
(grunts)

481
00:32:55.087 --> 00:32:57.456
Do you know where we are?

482
00:32:59.792 --> 00:33:02.926
Écoust is directly southeast.

483
00:33:02.928 --> 00:33:07.499
And if we keep that bearing.
we should make it.

484
00:33:11.570 --> 00:33:12.970
Come on. then.

485
00:33:12.972 --> 00:33:14.907
♪ ♪

486
00:33:22.815 --> 00:33:24.750
(stifled coughing)

487
00:33:37.730 --> 00:33:39.629
Look at that.

488
00:33:39.631 --> 00:33:42.599
They destroyed their own guns.

489
00:33:42.601 --> 00:33:45.802
SCHOFIELD: They destroyed
their own trenches. too.

490
00:33:45.804 --> 00:33:47.738
What do you mean?

491
00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:50.040
I think they wanted us
to go that way.

492
00:33:50.042 --> 00:33:52.011
They wanted to bury us.

493
00:33:58.984 --> 00:34:01.553
You bastard rats.

494
00:34:03.889 --> 00:34:05.591
(coughs. sniffs)

495
00:34:08.193 --> 00:34:09.926
Hey.

496
00:34:09.928 --> 00:34:12.162
Did you hear that story
about Wilko?

497
00:34:12.164 --> 00:34:15.032
- How he lost his ear.
- I'm not in the mood.

498
00:34:15.034 --> 00:34:16.800
Keep your eyes on the trees.

499
00:34:16.802 --> 00:34:18.771
Top of the ridge.

500
00:34:21.640 --> 00:34:23.575
Bet he told you
it was shrapnel.

501
00:34:25.144 --> 00:34:27.778
What was it. then?

502
00:34:27.780 --> 00:34:31.848
Well. you know his girl's
a hairdresser. right?

503
00:34:31.850 --> 00:34:34.184
And he was moaning about
the lack of bathing facilities

504
00:34:34.186 --> 00:34:36.387
when he wrote to her.

505
00:34:36.389 --> 00:34:38.121
Remember those
rancid jakes at Arras?

506
00:34:38.123 --> 00:34:39.756
Yeah.

507
00:34:39.758 --> 00:34:44.061
Anyway. she sends him over
this hair oil.

508
00:34:44.063 --> 00:34:47.066
Smells sweet.
Like Golden Syrup.

509
00:34:49.101 --> 00:34:52.035
Wilko loves the smell.

510
00:34:52.037 --> 00:34:55.705
but he doesn't want
to cart it around in his pack.

511
00:34:55.707 --> 00:35:01.178
So he slathers it all over
his barnet. goes to sleep.

512
00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:04.047
And in the middle of the night.
he wakes up.

513
00:35:04.049 --> 00:35:07.951
and a rat is sitting
on his shoulder.

514
00:35:07.953 --> 00:35:10.253
- licking the oil off his head.
- (chuckles)

515
00:35:10.255 --> 00:35:13.090
- Wilko panics. and he jumps up.
- (laughing)

516
00:35:13.092 --> 00:35:16.793
And when he does. the rat bites
clean through his fucking ear

517
00:35:16.795 --> 00:35:18.862
- and runs off with it.
- (laughs): No.

518
00:35:18.864 --> 00:35:21.064
Oh. he made a hell of a fuss...

519
00:35:21.066 --> 00:35:23.700
yelling. screaming.

520
00:35:23.702 --> 00:35:27.037
The best of it was he put
so much bloody oil on himself

521
00:35:27.039 --> 00:35:28.772
that he couldn't wash it off.

522
00:35:28.774 --> 00:35:30.040
He was like a magnet.

523
00:35:30.042 --> 00:35:31.775
The rats left us alone.

524
00:35:31.777 --> 00:35:33.710
but they couldn't get
enough of him. (chuckles)

525
00:35:33.712 --> 00:35:35.712
Poor bastard.

526
00:35:35.714 --> 00:35:37.649
(plane engines buzzing)

527
00:35:42.087 --> 00:35:44.754
Heading back home.

528
00:35:44.756 --> 00:35:48.225
I wonder what they saw.

529
00:35:48.227 --> 00:35:50.162
Watch the ridgelines.

530
00:35:54.867 --> 00:35:58.034
Well. that's your medal
sorted. then.

531
00:35:58.036 --> 00:36:00.036
What do you mean?

532
00:36:00.038 --> 00:36:02.005
"Lance Corporal Blake
showed unusual valor

533
00:36:02.007 --> 00:36:04.708
rescuing a comrade
from certain death."

534
00:36:04.710 --> 00:36:07.478
Blah. blah. blah.

535
00:36:07.480 --> 00:36:09.713
You reckon?

536
00:36:09.715 --> 00:36:11.049
I do.

537
00:36:13.752 --> 00:36:15.185
Well. that'd be nice.

538
00:36:15.187 --> 00:36:17.053
Since you lost yours.

539
00:36:17.055 --> 00:36:20.023
I didn't lose mine.

540
00:36:20.025 --> 00:36:21.892
What happened to it. then?

541
00:36:21.894 --> 00:36:24.830
- Why do you care?
- Why do you not?

542
00:36:26.899 --> 00:36:29.132
I swapped it
with a French captain.

543
00:36:29.134 --> 00:36:31.134
- Swapped it?
- Mm-hmm.

544
00:36:31.136 --> 00:36:32.869
For what?

545
00:36:32.871 --> 00:36:35.005
Bottle of wine.

546
00:36:35.007 --> 00:36:36.907
Well. what did you do that for?

547
00:36:36.909 --> 00:36:38.110
I was thirsty.

548
00:36:40.045 --> 00:36:41.813
What a waste.

549
00:36:43.849 --> 00:36:45.815
You should've
taken it home with you.

550
00:36:45.817 --> 00:36:48.287
You should've given it
to your family.

551
00:36:50.789 --> 00:36:53.256
Men have died for that.

552
00:36:53.258 --> 00:36:55.526
If I got a medal.
I'd take it back home.

553
00:36:55.528 --> 00:36:57.060
Why didn't you just
take it home?

554
00:36:57.062 --> 00:36:59.162
Look. it's just
a bit of bloody tin.

555
00:36:59.164 --> 00:37:00.997
It doesn't make you special.

556
00:37:00.999 --> 00:37:04.000
Doesn't make
any difference to anyone.

557
00:37:04.002 --> 00:37:06.036
Yes. it does.

558
00:37:06.038 --> 00:37:07.938
And it's not just a bit of tin.

559
00:37:07.940 --> 00:37:10.142
- It's got a ribbon on it.
- (chuckles)

560
00:37:14.246 --> 00:37:16.246
I hated going home.

561
00:37:16.248 --> 00:37:18.116
I hated it.

562
00:37:20.152 --> 00:37:23.019
When I knew I couldn't stay.

563
00:37:23.021 --> 00:37:25.991
when I knew I had to leave
and they might never see...

564
00:37:35.033 --> 00:37:37.002
♪ ♪

565
00:37:43.141 --> 00:37:45.010
(sighs)

566
00:37:53.419 --> 00:37:57.020
(birds chirping)

567
00:37:57.022 --> 00:37:58.257
Jesus.

568
00:38:01.193 --> 00:38:03.161
They chopped them all down.

569
00:38:07.065 --> 00:38:08.767
Cherries.

570
00:38:12.137 --> 00:38:13.939
Lamberts.

571
00:38:17.042 --> 00:38:19.109
They might be dukes.

572
00:38:19.111 --> 00:38:22.045
Hard to tell
when they aren't in fruit.

573
00:38:22.047 --> 00:38:24.314
What's the difference?

574
00:38:24.316 --> 00:38:27.183
Well. people think
there's one type.

575
00:38:27.185 --> 00:38:28.852
But there's lots of them.

576
00:38:28.854 --> 00:38:31.121
Cuthberts. Queen Annes.
Montmorencys.

577
00:38:31.123 --> 00:38:33.023
sweet ones. sour ones.

578
00:38:33.025 --> 00:38:35.393
Why on earth
would you know this?

579
00:38:35.395 --> 00:38:38.061
Mum's got an orchard back home.

580
00:38:38.063 --> 00:38:40.398
Only a few trees.

581
00:38:40.400 --> 00:38:43.867
This time of year. it looks
like it's been snowing.

582
00:38:43.869 --> 00:38:46.470
Blossom everywhere.

583
00:38:46.472 --> 00:38:50.073
And then in May.
we have to pick them.

584
00:38:50.075 --> 00:38:51.310
Me and Joe.

585
00:38:53.345 --> 00:38:55.912
Takes the whole day.

586
00:38:55.914 --> 00:38:58.281
So these ones are all goners?

587
00:38:58.283 --> 00:39:00.016
Oh. no.

588
00:39:00.018 --> 00:39:02.352
They'll grow again
when the stones rot.

589
00:39:02.354 --> 00:39:04.289
You'll end up with
more trees than before.

590
00:39:10.195 --> 00:39:11.995
It looks abandoned.

591
00:39:11.997 --> 00:39:14.230
Let's hope so.

592
00:39:14.232 --> 00:39:16.101
We have to make sure.

593
00:39:18.671 --> 00:39:20.671
♪ ♪

594
00:39:20.673 --> 00:39:22.841
(crows cawing in distance)

595
00:39:42.695 --> 00:39:44.329
♪ ♪

596
00:39:48.401 --> 00:39:50.470
(quietly):
I'll take front. You take back.

597
00:40:10.556 --> 00:40:12.492
♪ ♪

598
00:40:22.402 --> 00:40:24.336
(broken glass tinkles
under footstep)

599
00:40:33.178 --> 00:40:35.178
Anything?

600
00:40:35.180 --> 00:40:36.915
Nothing.

601
00:40:55.167 --> 00:40:57.403
- Did you find any food?
- No.

602
00:40:59.271 --> 00:41:01.206
I don't like this place.

603
00:41:21.293 --> 00:41:23.228
♪ ♪

604
00:41:29.067 --> 00:41:30.403
(cow moos in distance)

605
00:41:47.587 --> 00:41:49.522
(cow moos)

606
00:41:59.264 --> 00:42:00.466
(gasps softly)

607
00:42:05.538 --> 00:42:08.839
(sighs)

608
00:42:08.841 --> 00:42:12.510
Map says get over that ridge.

609
00:42:12.512 --> 00:42:16.145
and it's a straight shot
to Écoust.

610
00:42:16.147 --> 00:42:17.617
Good.

611
00:42:27.627 --> 00:42:29.562
(planes buzzing in distance)

612
00:42:44.410 --> 00:42:46.344
(rapid gunfire in distance)

613
00:42:57.523 --> 00:42:59.458
(rapid gunfire continues
in distance)

614
00:43:01.527 --> 00:43:04.193
BLAKE:
Is that our friends again?

615
00:43:04.195 --> 00:43:06.497
SCHOFIELD:
Looks like it.

616
00:43:06.499 --> 00:43:09.299
Dogfight.

617
00:43:09.301 --> 00:43:11.334
BLAKE:
Who's winning?

618
00:43:11.336 --> 00:43:13.470
SCHOFIELD:
Us. I think.

619
00:43:13.472 --> 00:43:15.206
Two on one.

620
00:43:16.542 --> 00:43:18.475
- (explosion in distance)
- (engine buzzing)

621
00:43:18.477 --> 00:43:20.413
BLAKE:
They got him.

622
00:43:33.158 --> 00:43:35.093
(engine sputtering)

623
00:43:44.570 --> 00:43:46.505
(pilot screaming)

624
00:43:53.579 --> 00:43:55.514
(pilot grunting)

625
00:44:05.591 --> 00:44:07.526
(pilot pleading in German)

626
00:44:11.731 --> 00:44:13.229
- (grunts)
- (coughs)

627
00:44:13.231 --> 00:44:15.166
(speaking German)

628
00:44:18.236 --> 00:44:19.770
We should put him
out of his misery.

629
00:44:19.772 --> 00:44:21.707
No. Get him some water.
He needs water.

630
00:44:23.609 --> 00:44:26.275
It's all right.
It's all right. Steady.

631
00:44:26.277 --> 00:44:29.247
- St-Steady. Stay still.
- PILOT: <i>Nein.</i>

632
00:44:32.685 --> 00:44:34.654
(pump creaking)

633
00:44:37.423 --> 00:44:39.690
Stop. Stop!

634
00:44:39.692 --> 00:44:41.625
- (Blake grunting)
- No. no. no!

635
00:44:41.627 --> 00:44:43.562
(Blake screams)

636
00:44:45.431 --> 00:44:47.531
No!

637
00:44:47.533 --> 00:44:49.265
You bastard!

638
00:44:49.267 --> 00:44:50.567
(groans)

639
00:44:50.569 --> 00:44:52.603
You bloody bastard.

640
00:44:52.605 --> 00:44:54.305
(groans)

641
00:44:56.008 --> 00:44:57.441
- (gasps)
- Oh.

642
00:44:57.443 --> 00:44:58.676
- Oh. God. no.
- Oh.

643
00:44:58.678 --> 00:45:00.276
My God. no!

644
00:45:00.278 --> 00:45:01.645
(groans)

645
00:45:01.647 --> 00:45:04.415
Jes... Jesus. no!

646
00:45:04.417 --> 00:45:06.315
- Oh. Je...
- We have to stop the bleeding.

647
00:45:06.317 --> 00:45:08.452
(cries out)
Stop! Stop it! Stop it!

648
00:45:08.454 --> 00:45:10.454
It's all right. It's all right.
It's going to be all right.

649
00:45:10.456 --> 00:45:11.655
Ow. (groans)

650
00:45:11.657 --> 00:45:13.524
- We're going to stand up.
- (groans)

651
00:45:13.526 --> 00:45:15.426
- Yes. Yes.
- Right?

652
00:45:15.428 --> 00:45:17.362
- Okay.
- (grunting)

653
00:45:19.565 --> 00:45:21.398
No! I can't. I can't.

654
00:45:21.400 --> 00:45:23.467
Ow! (panting)

655
00:45:23.469 --> 00:45:25.602
- We have to get to an aid post.
- I can't!

656
00:45:25.604 --> 00:45:27.303
I'll carry you.
It isn't very far.

657
00:45:27.305 --> 00:45:28.672
Just bring a doctor here.

658
00:45:28.674 --> 00:45:30.607
We can't.
We have to go together.

659
00:45:30.609 --> 00:45:32.275
(panting. groans)

660
00:45:32.277 --> 00:45:35.379
We're going to get up.
We're going to get up.

661
00:45:35.381 --> 00:45:38.216
(both grunting)

662
00:45:41.454 --> 00:45:43.286
Stop.

663
00:45:43.288 --> 00:45:44.755
Please stop!

664
00:45:44.757 --> 00:45:46.056
Put me down!

665
00:45:46.058 --> 00:45:47.624
Put me down. you bastard!

666
00:45:47.626 --> 00:45:49.660
Please! Put me down!

667
00:45:49.662 --> 00:45:51.327
(both grunting)

668
00:45:51.329 --> 00:45:53.264
- (Schofield panting)
- (Blake groaning)

669
00:45:57.703 --> 00:45:58.771
(Schofield sighs)

670
00:46:00.338 --> 00:46:01.905
You have to try to keep moving.

671
00:46:01.907 --> 00:46:03.607
Let's just sit.

672
00:46:03.609 --> 00:46:05.476
- Let me sit.
- No. we can't.

673
00:46:05.478 --> 00:46:07.411
We have to find the 2nd.
remember?

674
00:46:07.413 --> 00:46:08.779
Your brother.
We have to go now.

675
00:46:08.781 --> 00:46:10.714
You can start on without me.
I'll catch up.

676
00:46:10.716 --> 00:46:11.715
No.

677
00:46:11.717 --> 00:46:13.684
You can't stay here.

678
00:46:13.686 --> 00:46:15.819
We have to move.
All right?

679
00:46:15.821 --> 00:46:17.454
We have to move.

680
00:46:17.456 --> 00:46:19.355
Come on. Come on!

681
00:46:19.357 --> 00:46:21.391
- (groans)
- Come on.

682
00:46:21.393 --> 00:46:23.326
That's it. Come on.

683
00:46:23.328 --> 00:46:26.697
- Come on. Come on.
- (groans)

684
00:46:26.699 --> 00:46:28.667
(crying out)

685
00:46:30.903 --> 00:46:32.402
Your brother.

686
00:46:32.404 --> 00:46:34.404
We have to find your brother.

687
00:46:34.406 --> 00:46:36.673
(both panting)

688
00:46:36.675 --> 00:46:39.676
You'll recognize him.

689
00:46:39.678 --> 00:46:41.645
He looks like me.

690
00:46:41.647 --> 00:46:44.114
And he's a bit older.

691
00:46:44.116 --> 00:46:45.417
(wood creaks)

692
00:46:48.821 --> 00:46:50.756
(panting)

693
00:46:56.896 --> 00:46:58.697
What are they?

694
00:47:00.766 --> 00:47:03.767
Are we being shelled?

695
00:47:03.769 --> 00:47:05.402
They're embers.

696
00:47:05.404 --> 00:47:07.338
The barn is on fire.

697
00:47:11.443 --> 00:47:12.778
I've been hit.

698
00:47:14.847 --> 00:47:17.581
What was it?

699
00:47:17.583 --> 00:47:19.317
You were stabbed.

700
00:47:24.757 --> 00:47:26.725
Am I dying?

701
00:47:34.934 --> 00:47:37.868
Yes.

702
00:47:37.870 --> 00:47:39.805
Yes. I think you are.

703
00:47:43.809 --> 00:47:47.544
(panting):
Oh. No. No.

704
00:47:47.546 --> 00:47:49.481
(panting softly)

705
00:47:56.722 --> 00:47:58.188
This?

706
00:47:58.190 --> 00:47:59.823
Inside.

707
00:47:59.825 --> 00:48:01.760
♪ ♪

708
00:48:23.616 --> 00:48:26.984
Will you write
to my mum for me?

709
00:48:26.986 --> 00:48:28.687
I will.

710
00:48:31.523 --> 00:48:33.523
Tell her I wasn't scared.

711
00:48:33.525 --> 00:48:34.960
(sobbing softly)

712
00:48:37.029 --> 00:48:39.229
Anything else?

713
00:48:39.231 --> 00:48:41.031
(panting)

714
00:48:41.033 --> 00:48:42.868
I love them.

715
00:48:44.937 --> 00:48:46.705
I wish that...

716
00:48:48.774 --> 00:48:50.709
I wish that...

717
00:48:56.248 --> 00:48:57.816
Talk to me.

718
00:49:00.052 --> 00:49:01.987
Tell me you know the way.

719
00:49:05.591 --> 00:49:08.592
I know the way.

720
00:49:08.594 --> 00:49:11.962
I'm going to head southeast
until I hit Écoust.

721
00:49:11.964 --> 00:49:14.932
I'll pass through the town
and out to the east.

722
00:49:14.934 --> 00:49:17.634
all the way to Croisilles Wood.

723
00:49:17.636 --> 00:49:20.103
It'll be dark by then.

724
00:49:20.105 --> 00:49:22.739
That won't bother me.

725
00:49:22.741 --> 00:49:24.741
I'll find the 2nd.

726
00:49:24.743 --> 00:49:26.810
I'll give them the message.

727
00:49:26.812 --> 00:49:29.079
and then I'll find
your brother.

728
00:49:29.081 --> 00:49:30.881
Just like you.

729
00:49:30.883 --> 00:49:32.851
A little older.

730
00:49:36.922 --> 00:49:38.857
(wind whistling softly)

731
00:50:03.716 --> 00:50:05.050
(exhales softly)

732
00:50:41.620 --> 00:50:43.555
(metal clinking quietly)

733
00:51:10.149 --> 00:51:11.850
(sighs)

734
00:51:25.164 --> 00:51:27.099
(grunting)

735
00:51:35.074 --> 00:51:37.974
PARRY:
You all right. mate?

736
00:51:37.976 --> 00:51:40.911
Hey. it's all right.
It's okay.

737
00:51:40.913 --> 00:51:42.748
Come on. help him.

738
00:51:47.820 --> 00:51:49.755
(men grunt)

739
00:51:51.824 --> 00:51:55.992
Jesus. What happened to him?

740
00:51:55.994 --> 00:51:57.727
Was it the plane?

741
00:51:57.729 --> 00:51:59.932
- We saw the smoke.
- Yeah.

742
00:52:04.970 --> 00:52:07.237
(all grunting)

743
00:52:07.239 --> 00:52:09.174
(Schofield panting)

744
00:52:16.982 --> 00:52:18.849
SMITH:
Go and fetch his things.

745
00:52:18.851 --> 00:52:20.686
- PARRY: Sir.
- ATKINS: Sir.

746
00:52:24.123 --> 00:52:25.824
SMITH:
A friend?

747
00:52:28.760 --> 00:52:30.894
What are you doing here?

748
00:52:30.896 --> 00:52:33.730
I have an urgent message
for the 2nd Devons.

749
00:52:33.732 --> 00:52:36.133
Orders to stop
tomorrow morning's attack.

750
00:52:36.135 --> 00:52:38.034
And where are they stationed?

751
00:52:38.036 --> 00:52:40.471
Just beyond Écoust.

752
00:52:40.473 --> 00:52:42.040
Come with me.

753
00:52:48.147 --> 00:52:50.914
Come with me. Corporal.
It's an order.

754
00:52:50.916 --> 00:52:52.048
We're passing through Écoust.

755
00:52:52.050 --> 00:52:53.986
We can take you
some of the way.

756
00:52:55.921 --> 00:52:57.122
Sir.

757
00:53:03.896 --> 00:53:05.831
♪ ♪

758
00:53:17.309 --> 00:53:19.309
(man coughing)

759
00:53:19.311 --> 00:53:22.179
(engine idling nearby)

760
00:53:22.181 --> 00:53:23.914
COLLINS:
Oh. come on. Sergeant!

761
00:53:23.916 --> 00:53:26.183
Put more men on the base.

762
00:53:26.185 --> 00:53:29.019
At the trunk.
It'll be heavier there.

763
00:53:29.021 --> 00:53:30.887
Might be a tight squeeze.

764
00:53:30.889 --> 00:53:33.223
COLLINS: No. you're not going
to be able to just lift it.

765
00:53:33.225 --> 00:53:35.825
Pivot the front end
to the left.

766
00:53:35.827 --> 00:53:38.128
Jesus. they don't
make things easy. do they?

767
00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:40.964
They could at least have
retreated with a bit of grace.

768
00:53:40.966 --> 00:53:42.065
Bastards!

769
00:53:42.067 --> 00:53:43.833
Sir.

770
00:53:43.835 --> 00:53:45.835
- You're not one of mine.
- No. sir.

771
00:53:45.837 --> 00:53:48.305
He has an urgent message to
deliver to the 2nd Devons. sir.

772
00:53:48.307 --> 00:53:49.906
COLLINS:
Can you get past it?

773
00:53:49.908 --> 00:53:51.375
- HARROP: No. sir.
- Oh. for God's sake.

774
00:53:51.377 --> 00:53:53.843
Just move it!

775
00:53:53.845 --> 00:53:56.346
There's room
in the casuals' truck. sir.

776
00:53:56.348 --> 00:53:58.848
- He has orders.
- Yes. yes. all right.

777
00:53:58.850 --> 00:54:01.851
Come on. now. You can
get through there sideways.

778
00:54:01.853 --> 00:54:03.789
(engine revving)

779
00:54:06.091 --> 00:54:08.091
How did you get here. sir?

780
00:54:08.093 --> 00:54:11.861
Crossed no-man's-land.
just outside Bapaume.

781
00:54:11.863 --> 00:54:13.330
Took us the whole night.

782
00:54:13.332 --> 00:54:15.832
Bumped into a couple of
Hun stragglers on the way

783
00:54:15.834 --> 00:54:18.034
who made a nuisance
of themselves.

784
00:54:18.036 --> 00:54:19.869
You're going up
to the new line?

785
00:54:19.871 --> 00:54:21.338
Attempting to.

786
00:54:21.340 --> 00:54:23.073
The Newfoundlands
have pushed forwards

787
00:54:23.075 --> 00:54:25.010
and requested reinforcements.

788
00:54:27.079 --> 00:54:30.146
I'm sorry about your friend.

789
00:54:30.148 --> 00:54:32.985
May I tell you something
that you probably already know?

790
00:54:35.020 --> 00:54:38.021
It doesn't do to dwell on it.

791
00:54:38.023 --> 00:54:39.990
No. sir.

792
00:54:39.992 --> 00:54:41.258
Hop on.

793
00:54:41.260 --> 00:54:43.093
(men chattering)

794
00:54:43.095 --> 00:54:44.930
Make some space there.

795
00:54:46.898 --> 00:54:48.965
Come on. in you get.

796
00:54:48.967 --> 00:54:51.968
♪ ♪

797
00:54:51.970 --> 00:54:53.171
(Schofield grunts)

798
00:55:04.916 --> 00:55:07.786
All right.
Here we go again. boys.

799
00:55:10.188 --> 00:55:13.792
Welcome aboard the night bus
to fuck-knows-where.

800
00:55:17.129 --> 00:55:20.297
Is that a dead dog?

801
00:55:20.299 --> 00:55:22.299
- You got a fag?
- Yeah. There you go.

802
00:55:22.301 --> 00:55:24.236
♪ ♪

803
00:55:38.984 --> 00:55:40.350
- Butler.
- BUTLER: Oy.

804
00:55:40.352 --> 00:55:43.153
- Carry on with that story.
- Oh. yeah. Right.

805
00:55:43.155 --> 00:55:47.290
So. when we get off the train.
Beaufoy comes up to us.

806
00:55:47.292 --> 00:55:49.959
and he's having a right go.

807
00:55:49.961 --> 00:55:51.928
(lisping): "Lance Corporal.
whatever one does.

808
00:55:51.930 --> 00:55:53.397
- one never lets standards slip."
- (chuckles)

809
00:55:53.399 --> 00:55:55.265
Then Scott comes
out the latrine.

810
00:55:55.267 --> 00:55:58.034
He wipes his hand on the back
of Beaufoy's jacket.

811
00:55:58.036 --> 00:56:00.303
- Shit all down his back.
- (laughs)

812
00:56:00.305 --> 00:56:02.005
Was that meant to be
Captain Beaufoy?

813
00:56:02.007 --> 00:56:05.075
BUTLER: Oh. piss off. you.
You can't do any better.

814
00:56:05.077 --> 00:56:06.677
(lisping):
"Men. your rifle stocks are

815
00:56:06.679 --> 00:56:09.245
an embarrassment to the entire
Expeditionary Force."

816
00:56:09.247 --> 00:56:10.514
- BUTLER: Nah.
- MALKY: No.

817
00:56:10.516 --> 00:56:12.315
JONDALAR:
You're both bloody awful.

818
00:56:12.317 --> 00:56:14.984
You don't know. You barely even
speak the bloody language.

819
00:56:14.986 --> 00:56:16.986
MALKY: He's got a better grasp
of it than you. Cooke.

820
00:56:16.988 --> 00:56:19.022
Well. go on. then. Jondalar.
Give it a go. Let's see it.

821
00:56:19.024 --> 00:56:21.257
(others murmuring
encouragement)

822
00:56:21.259 --> 00:56:25.061
"Rossi! Never in my 200 years
as a soldier

823
00:56:25.063 --> 00:56:27.498
have I seen such a sorry excuse
for a latrine pit."

824
00:56:27.500 --> 00:56:29.265
- (laughter)
- Shite.

825
00:56:29.267 --> 00:56:31.469
That is total shit.

826
00:56:31.471 --> 00:56:33.504
Oy. Could've taken
my teeth out with that.

827
00:56:33.506 --> 00:56:35.441
MALKY:
You could do with a new set.

828
00:56:40.480 --> 00:56:43.514
BUTLER: You got somewhere
you need to be?

829
00:56:43.516 --> 00:56:45.048
- (truck creaks)
- (men groan)

830
00:56:45.050 --> 00:56:47.283
- No.
- Arsehole needs driving lessons.

831
00:56:47.285 --> 00:56:50.721
(horn honking)

832
00:56:50.723 --> 00:56:53.892
- (men shouting in distance)
- (horn honking in distance)

833
00:56:57.730 --> 00:56:59.898
(engine revving)

834
00:57:03.101 --> 00:57:06.369
- He should reverse.
- COOKE: Yeah.

835
00:57:06.371 --> 00:57:08.439
Try it in reverse.

836
00:57:08.441 --> 00:57:10.173
- Reverse!
- (gear creaks)

837
00:57:10.175 --> 00:57:12.144
(engine revving)

838
00:57:17.315 --> 00:57:18.382
No. Stop.

839
00:57:18.384 --> 00:57:20.216
- Stop!
- (revving stops)

840
00:57:20.218 --> 00:57:22.486
- Everyone needs to get out.
- BUTLER: Who the fuck is he?

841
00:57:22.488 --> 00:57:25.389
All out!

842
00:57:25.391 --> 00:57:27.023
- Come on!
- All right.

843
00:57:27.025 --> 00:57:30.026
All right.
keep your bloody hair on.

844
00:57:30.028 --> 00:57:31.429
Right.

845
00:57:31.431 --> 00:57:34.264
One. two. three!

846
00:57:34.266 --> 00:57:36.201
(engine revving)

847
00:57:39.104 --> 00:57:41.070
- (grunts. sighs)
- (revving stops)

848
00:57:41.072 --> 00:57:43.239
We need to get some wood.
put it under the wheels.

849
00:57:43.241 --> 00:57:44.442
No. we haven't got the time.

850
00:57:44.444 --> 00:57:46.477
We all need to push.
Come on.

851
00:57:46.479 --> 00:57:48.044
Come on!

852
00:57:48.046 --> 00:57:50.348
- (engine revving)
- (Schofield yells)

853
00:57:53.351 --> 00:57:54.350
(revving stops)

854
00:57:54.352 --> 00:57:56.587
Please. I have to go now.

855
00:57:56.589 --> 00:57:59.490
Please.

856
00:57:59.492 --> 00:58:01.224
All right. come on. lads.

857
00:58:01.226 --> 00:58:02.225
Come on.

858
00:58:02.227 --> 00:58:03.326
ROSSI:
Come on. boys.

859
00:58:03.328 --> 00:58:06.329
One. two. three.

860
00:58:06.331 --> 00:58:08.266
(engine revving)

861
00:58:10.503 --> 00:58:13.138
(Schofield yells)

862
00:58:15.106 --> 00:58:17.340
- (yells)
- (revving stops)

863
00:58:17.342 --> 00:58:19.510
ROSSI: Come on. boys!
One last push. yeah?!

864
00:58:19.512 --> 00:58:22.312
One. two. three!

865
00:58:22.314 --> 00:58:24.249
- (engine revving)
- (men straining)

866
00:58:29.455 --> 00:58:32.391
(men sighing. cheering.
laughing)

867
00:58:34.594 --> 00:58:36.226
Back in.

868
00:58:36.228 --> 00:58:37.595
Get back in.

869
00:58:37.597 --> 00:58:39.530
Go. (panting)

870
00:58:39.532 --> 00:58:41.599
Are you all right?

871
00:58:41.601 --> 00:58:43.366
Here. driver. how about you try

872
00:58:43.368 --> 00:58:45.536
to keep it on the bloody road
for a change?

873
00:58:45.538 --> 00:58:47.270
DRIVER:
Oh. piss off.

874
00:58:47.272 --> 00:58:49.272
(men chattering)

875
00:58:49.274 --> 00:58:51.209
(Schofield panting)

876
00:59:06.157 --> 00:59:09.259
So. where are you going?

877
00:59:09.261 --> 00:59:13.096
I have to get to the
2nd Devons. just past Écoust.

878
00:59:13.098 --> 00:59:15.265
Why?

879
00:59:15.267 --> 00:59:16.867
They're attacking at dawn.

880
00:59:16.869 --> 00:59:18.569
I have orders to stop them.

881
00:59:18.571 --> 00:59:21.204
How come?

882
00:59:21.206 --> 00:59:23.172
They're walking into a trap.

883
00:59:23.174 --> 00:59:25.174
How many?

884
00:59:25.176 --> 00:59:26.377
Sixteen hundred.

885
00:59:26.379 --> 00:59:28.344
Ooh. (exhales sharply)

886
00:59:28.346 --> 00:59:30.048
Jesus.

887
00:59:32.117 --> 00:59:35.218
Why did they send you
on your own?

888
00:59:35.220 --> 00:59:36.620
They didn't.

889
00:59:36.622 --> 00:59:39.355
There were two of us.

890
00:59:39.357 --> 00:59:41.525
So now it's down to you?

891
00:59:41.527 --> 00:59:44.193
Yes.

892
00:59:44.195 --> 00:59:46.131
You'll never make it.

893
00:59:49.602 --> 00:59:51.537
SCHOFIELD:
Yes. I will.

894
00:59:57.610 --> 00:59:59.610
Thank you.

895
00:59:59.612 --> 01:00:01.645
♪ ♪

896
01:00:01.647 --> 01:00:03.614
Look at it.

897
01:00:03.616 --> 01:00:06.417
Fucking look at it.

898
01:00:06.419 --> 01:00:09.285
Three years fighting over this.

899
01:00:09.287 --> 01:00:12.255
We should've just let
the bastards keep it.

900
01:00:12.257 --> 01:00:14.190
I mean. who machine-guns cows?

901
01:00:14.192 --> 01:00:16.427
Huns with extra bullets.

902
01:00:16.429 --> 01:00:18.495
Bastards.

903
01:00:18.497 --> 01:00:20.330
Clever.

904
01:00:20.332 --> 01:00:24.635
They know if they don't shoot
the cow. you will eat it.

905
01:00:24.637 --> 01:00:26.336
ROSSI:
Still bastards.

906
01:00:26.338 --> 01:00:28.739
MALKY: Yeah. it's not even
our bloody country.

907
01:00:28.741 --> 01:00:30.674
How long gone
do you reckon they are?

908
01:00:30.676 --> 01:00:32.208
JONDALAR:
Why?

909
01:00:32.210 --> 01:00:33.577
Worried we'll catch up
with them?

910
01:00:33.579 --> 01:00:35.211
- (Malky chuckles)
- ROSSI: Yeah.

911
01:00:35.213 --> 01:00:37.414
Right. Be a bloody miracle
at this rate.

912
01:00:37.416 --> 01:00:39.650
COOKE: Yeah. they're probably
right round the next corner.

913
01:00:39.652 --> 01:00:42.486
ROSSI:
Piss off. No. they're not.

914
01:00:42.488 --> 01:00:45.188
Why don't they just
bloody well give up?

915
01:00:45.190 --> 01:00:47.791
Eh? Don't they want to go home?

916
01:00:47.793 --> 01:00:50.259
ROSSI: They hate
their wives and mothers.

917
01:00:50.261 --> 01:00:52.261
And Germany must be a shithole.

918
01:00:52.263 --> 01:00:54.263
(Malky chuckles)

919
01:00:54.265 --> 01:00:55.633
They're retreating.

920
01:00:55.635 --> 01:00:57.468
They're miles back.

921
01:00:57.470 --> 01:01:00.269
Got 'em on the ropes. at least.

922
01:01:00.271 --> 01:01:03.641
JONDALAR:
No. We don't.

923
01:01:03.643 --> 01:01:05.409
(horn honks)

924
01:01:05.411 --> 01:01:07.276
Oh. bollocks.
What's up now?

925
01:01:07.278 --> 01:01:08.812
- Not another bloody tree.
- (brakes squeaking)

926
01:01:08.814 --> 01:01:10.414
DRIVER:
Bridge is down.

927
01:01:10.416 --> 01:01:12.650
Oh. That's a shame.

928
01:01:12.652 --> 01:01:14.685
Looks like I'll be
getting out here.

929
01:01:14.687 --> 01:01:16.587
Good luck.

930
01:01:16.589 --> 01:01:18.422
ROSSI: Keep some of that luck
for yourself. pal.

931
01:01:18.424 --> 01:01:20.289
- Think you'll be needing it.
- BUTLER: Good luck. mate.

932
01:01:20.291 --> 01:01:21.558
- MALKY: Good luck.
- COOKE: Good luck. gov.

933
01:01:21.560 --> 01:01:23.694
- SINGER: Good luck.
- Don't balls it up.

934
01:01:23.696 --> 01:01:26.597
I hope you get there.

935
01:01:26.599 --> 01:01:28.299
Thank you.

936
01:01:31.537 --> 01:01:33.336
The next bridge is six miles.

937
01:01:33.338 --> 01:01:34.705
We'll have to divert.

938
01:01:34.707 --> 01:01:36.407
I can't. sir.

939
01:01:36.409 --> 01:01:38.341
I don't have the time.

940
01:01:38.343 --> 01:01:41.344
Of course.

941
01:01:41.346 --> 01:01:43.414
Best of luck.

942
01:01:43.416 --> 01:01:44.750
Thank you. sir.

943
01:01:48.353 --> 01:01:50.387
Corporal?

944
01:01:50.389 --> 01:01:54.323
If you do manage to get
to Colonel Mackenzie.

945
01:01:54.325 --> 01:01:56.794
make sure there are witnesses.

946
01:01:56.796 --> 01:01:58.495
They are direct orders. sir.

947
01:01:58.497 --> 01:02:00.764
I know.

948
01:02:00.766 --> 01:02:03.400
But some men
just want the fight.

949
01:02:03.402 --> 01:02:05.636
♪ ♪

950
01:02:05.638 --> 01:02:07.338
Thank you. sir.

951
01:02:11.510 --> 01:02:14.345
SMITH:
Driver! Move off!

952
01:02:20.453 --> 01:02:22.453
(crows cawing)

953
01:02:22.455 --> 01:02:24.455
